    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2f75e3cc4fd6b2006d39212ed9602e184e25d19dee6d9fd82dbfff9683d8a586",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Prime-log resonance first enters the smooth test-function law through the lower-order density g_alpha. The discrepancy statistic then selects the interval on which that signed correction is largest; mollification transfers the smooth law to the existence of a sharp interval witness, while extremal trigonometric brackets decompose a general interval count into approximation error, arithmetically resolved low modes, and an unresolved high-frequency zero-sum tail.",
