    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_386dade7dc08d7a057ca8dcc21c93f651f48744758f657d573c8edfe7d7d3159",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The linear zero sum sum x^{i gamma} is a shared interface among three representations. Fourier expansion makes it the mode data for fractional-part interval counts; explicit-formula arguments transfer between its cancellation and short multiplicative increments of the prime-counting error; and a smoothed quadratic pair statistic bounds the linear sum through multiplicative second differences of normalized pair correlation.",
